Z||000010||DRH||09-17-2001||12:19 AM||||This hit and run crap has to stop. What did we do in Iraq? We have Hussein telling the people we are the reason they are miserable. We bomb the crap out of them and make them more miserable. Then we leave him there to CONTINUE telling them we are the problem. AND, we abandoned the rebels that might have overthrown him and let him exterminate most of them. And we wonder why they think we're the bad guys???<P>We need to eliminate certain bad influences over there with as little collateral damage as is practical. Then we need to help the ones that are left to rebuild. Japan hated us ever bit as much as these people during WW2. Why don't they hate us now? Because we helped them rebuild (almost did TOO good a job of that) and they SAW we weren't the bad guys.<P>The constant civil wars and poverty is a perfect breeding ground from which people like bin Laden and the Taliban have no problem recruiting terrorist. Until the conditions that these people feed off of are eliminated, terrorism  will be with us. It's not the race or the religion. It's the fact that desperate people are easily manipulated by madmen. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mach10||09-17-2001||02:46 AM||||Grinthock: This thread seems pretty clean. If it gets out of hand, I'll personally email cliff an appology and request it's deletion. This is a perspective from someone who knows a lot more about the Taliban/Islam than we do. I think that this is a good thing, that's why I posted it...<P>DRH: Well put. <P>Ken: The US is in no immediate military danger from the Middle east. That's why the fanatics have had to use terrorist tactics. As much as I am opposed to it, the only real solution MIGHT be to go in, topple the government, and start a new one. The people will not thank you for it, at least not right away, but once they get used to thinking for themselves, things might actually turn out OK.<BR>Not to put too fine a point on it, but the US must be very careful in the near future. The price must be paid, but the US will have to be careful collecting it. I'm hoping that the US can outsmart those rabid fanatic animals, and turn this whole situation against them.<P>Cali-kid: As a sort-of cultural anthropologist, I have to morally disagree. However, as a free-thinking individual who doesn't relish the thought of having his plane crash through a US civic building en route to Europe, I will have to agree. The time has come to give the message: Terrorism will not be tolerated. If it means the death of thousands of taliban child-soldiers, so be it. Though my heart is heavy to say it, that price to be paid is not higher than the cost of *innocent* lives back home. <P>------------------<BR>"Duct Tape is Good to Mach 3"<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.escape.ca/~fogg/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||09-17-2001||09:34 AM||||one way this whole thing can be stopped is through an information campain - Islamic leaders throughout the world who speak up and point out that these terrorist are using the faith of the people that follow them for their own personal gain - not to promote the interests of islam.<P> Nobody in america believe David Korey was Jesus - there was no anti Jesus backlash in america after Waco - only a handfull took issue against the way Waco was handled and stuck out at Oklamhoma city - but there is no indication that McVeigh was a follower of Korey.<P>  But in these islamic countries they dont seem to understand they and their religion is being perverted and they are being 'had' by these fanatics.<P>  that can be changed - even the people in the terrorist cells will see the light and stop their campains when they realize they have been following a con artist.<P>  bin laden is no more of an islamic holy man than hitler was an german.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Wannabe||09-17-2001||10:46 AM||||The process of eliminating or controlling this enemy is going to be far more complex than a "traditional" military stike. The enemy doesn't care if they die, and in fact looks forward to it. so the threat of death is not a deterrent. There are many of them, they are scattered everywhere and they move around all the time. The ultimate solution can only be reached by getting at the root of the problem. It's like trying to get rid of fleas with a fly swatter: you may kill lots of them with each swipe, but they have eggs hatching in the rugs, and there are more of them being carried all over the place by unsuspecting animals. It is similar to the war on drugs. After many year of guns, law enforcement and money spent, it is still a problem, and it the only solution in a free world is to change attitudes or conditions so that people no longer want to use drugs. In the case of the war on Terrorism, the world needs to find a way to changes these people's attitudes. As satisfying as a brutal military  stike might be, it probably won't do much to solve the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000014||stsmithgt||09-17-2001||11:29 AM||||I can almost compare the afganistan stand point with OLD Germany, but I don't see any ovens smoking over there full of people.  That leaves people that disagree with Laden, people that support him, one that don't want to have anything to do with it, and they're all mixed in together.  Unfortunatley, there's no way of separating them apart, especailly with the Laden gang scrambling amongst them.  That leaves some unsettling options.  I expect a focused and brutal attack not only from the air, but also on the ground with more casualties than I care to imagine on both sides.  Let it happen and get it over with even if it does take years.  In the <I>LONGER</I> run, it will be a better and safer place to live.  It'll give kids something to read about in there history class. (shallow comment)<P>Note: <BR>Not all kids will stay innocent victoms through this war.... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/terror045.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||Voytek||09-17-2001||12:21 PM||||In the Nazi Germany there were also innocent people who wanted nothing to do with Hitler.  Unfortunatly many of them died too and it was the only way to defeat Hitler.  In any war there are always civilian casualties - let's hope our leaders make the right choices and limit the number of dead to a minimum.<P>We do need to respond to this attack - there is no other way around it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mach10||09-17-2001||12:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>By stsmithgt</B><BR>Note: <BR>Not all kids will stay innocent victoms through this war....<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's my point exactly. Any war that the US engages with the islam will have our soldiers facing ranks and ranks of these little guys with AKs.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> <P>It's not going to be pretty.<P>About comparing the Taliban to the third riech, remember that it was only later that the Nazis started to upshift on the atrocities. At the moment, you can EASILY draw parrallels. Something MUST be done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
