Z||000020||Black88GT||09-18-2001||10:37 PM||||As evil as they were, the Nazi's was smart, innovative, and most importantly, WELL ORGANIZED.<P>Tailban is neither.  What economy have they built?  What advances in technology have they revelled in?  Do they have a chain of command?  Famous generals?<P>Remember, the US Space program (nasa) can thank the Nazi's for all of their rocket early technology, as can the Russians.  The early jet engine technology also came from the Nazi's.  <P>Tailban is a group of rag tag islamic militants who took over a 3rd world country already in shambles.  I'm not impressed. <P>Lastly, The Nazi's did not blame all their problems on Jews and neither do the Tailban.  Western culture is bigger then the jews.  Remember, there's a lot more people in this country then there are in Jerusalem.  <P>Many cultures wanted to take over the world, so that "similarity" isn't so great.  Neither is the conformity, considering you can say that about China and many other middle eastern countries.  I hope you have more similarities then that.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Mach10||09-18-2001||11:40 PM||||Actually, the Taliban is fairly well organized. As for unimpressive, well, they are managing to control several countries with nothing more than a few teenagers with assault rifles. That's pretty neat.<P>I don't know, the similarities shown are pretty much all you need to draw parrallels. Obviously the Talibans aren't going to shave their heads and tattoo swastikas to their asses. They ARE 2 completely different cultural phenomena. But, since you wanted similarities, I will recap, compare, and contrast:<P>-Taliban has goals of Global Domination. Islam does teach that everyone should idealliy be converted. Of course, like most everything else in the Qua'Ran, it's vague enough to mean just about anything from "preach" to "exterminate"<BR>-Hitler's Regime had plans of global domination. Of course, his plans were more straight-cut. High-tech and lots of tanks...<P>-The taliban teaches of racial superiority. Muslims are better, because they are "Gods People." Everyone else is lower than scum, or else consorting with the devil.<BR>-So did Hitler. Hitler's excuse, though (if you've read Mein Kamff) is that he was a bloody stupid lunatic.<P>-Taliban holds far-right despotic views (read: Theocracy). "we are in, you have no choice. Yeild to allah's (our) will, NOW.<BR>-Hitler was a despot, and held rightist views. "We are the superior race. Disagreement means that you aren't one of us, and therefore forfeit all rights"<P><BR>-Taliban represses/segregates those who's views aren't consistant with theirs. Foreiners are marginally tolerated in some cases, but mostly are forced to comply with their own views. <BR>-Hitler did this at first. It was only later that the attrocities started.<P>-Taliban forces (or convinces) people to do things that they know are wrong. They justify it by what their spiritual/political leaders say is what God wants<BR>-Hitler did also but his people justified it differently. For the SS, it was "following orders."<P>-The Taliban's popularity is somewhat forced. People are scared to offer any opposition, due to spiritual and physical conflicts/restraint.<BR>-The Nazi party was popular, as it offered hope, but not unanimous. When it became clear that they were very willing to ostracise anybody opposed (or different) to them, everybody waved the flag, if only to be left alone...<P>I don't know, but when you put it all together, they look pretty similar... However, to be fair, this is how they are different:<P>-Nazi party gained popularity because the German people were desparate for any way out of the hole that was dug for them in WWi. Hitler, with his silver speeches, and promises of a better way of life tugged right at the people's heartstrings. All of the things that made the National Socialist party evil came later. They originally stood for hope and prosperity.<BR>-The Taliban formed from a bunch of religious extremists, bent on stamping out all forms of evil, to form the perfect society as laid out in the Qua'Ran. The Taliban rose to power with a rabble of moderatly armed fanatics. Most of the people in the middle east AREN'T in the stone age. They are still in the Agricultural Revolution age. Most of the people are subsistance farmers. They really don't care about politics. They are more interested in the next harvest. They would offer little resistance, and would be unwilling to voice any concerns, even when the taliban started to squeeze. The taliban increased in power only as it got more members, and thus, more resources. Now, they are a fairly well organized, but spread out force.<P>-The Nazis, although christian in faith, were not a religiously oriented party. Their main concern was to get Germany to the top of the cream any way possible. This meant clearing out the Riff-raff to "purify" their society. (and coincidently absorbing the none-too small fortunes of the jewish population into their coffers)<BR>-The Talibans are Islamic. (No, Really?!? They are almost the DEFINITION [sadly] of Islam, now...) The funny part about the Qua'Ran is that it is almost not so much of a religious book than a How-To manual about running a perfect muslim society. This is why the Middle east hasn't moved much, in terms of technology (at least in the rural areas). Most governments don't feel the need to even bother with a separate bill of laws/rights and freedoms. It's all there in the Qua'Ran. They just use Islamic law. Everythings covered, from property rights to International Politics. Trouble is, the Qua'Ran is pretty vague about a lot of points, and the Taliban is apparantly VERY good at interpreting these things to mean some pretty extreme things. For example, Jihads are justified in the Qua'Ran. But from my own interpretation, it seems that Jihads are only appropriate when the enemy is attacking directly holy sites/people. The rest of the Qua'ran seems to teach "If attacked, fight to the death, but if the enemey runs away, desist immediately, fo he has seen the wisdom of Allah" I'll have to pull the referece chapter/verse later at home...<P><BR>I'm tired. I'll think of some more things later, after I've gotten some responses.<P>||||reg||7
Z||000022||Black88GT||09-18-2001||11:55 PM||||&lt;&lt;<I>Actually, the Taliban is fairly well organized. As for unimpressive, well, they are managing to control several countries with nothing more than a few teenagers with assault rifles. That's pretty neat.</I> &gt;&gt;<P>Umm, what countries, and what teenagers?  The Tailban showed on CNN were all old muslims with foot long beards.  How are they well organized?  If they are so well organized, how come they meet in mud huts and can't seem to make any kind of a country out of what they have.<P>&lt;&lt; <I>-Taliban has goals of Global Domination. Islam does teach that everyone should idealliy be converted. Of course, like most everything else in the Qua'Ran, it's vague enough to mean just about anything from "preach" to "exterminate"<BR>-Hitler's Regime had plans of global domination. Of course, his plans were more straight-cut. High-tech and lots of tanks...</I> &gt;&gt;<P>Russians preached global domination too (never backed up the threat though).  So did the Romans.  And the Greeks.  I could probably find 5 more if I tried.<P>&lt;&lt; <I>-The taliban teaches of racial superiority. Muslims are better, because they are "Gods People." Everyone else is lower than scum, or else consorting with the devil.<BR>-So did Hitler. Hitler's excuse, though (if you've read Mein Kamff) is that he was a bloody stupid lunatic.</I> &gt;&gt;<P>Many organizations/gov'ts preach that or have preached that.  Again, Romans, Greeks, I could go on.   Also I think you mean Mein Kempf, and yes I have read it.<P>&lt;&lt; <I>-Taliban holds far-right despotic views (read: Theocracy). "we are in, you have no choice. Yeild to allah's (our) will, NOW.<BR>-Hitler was a despot, and held rightist views. "We are the superior race. Disagreement means that you aren't one of us, and therefore forfeit all rights"</I> &gt;&gt;<P>China, Cuba, Vietnam, Sudan, Iraq, Iran etc. etc. etc.  Again, no unique similarity here.<P>&lt;&lt; <I>-Taliban represses/segregates those who's views aren't consistant with theirs. Foreiners are marginally tolerated in some cases, but mostly are forced to comply with their own views. <BR>-Hitler did this at first. It was only later that the attrocities started.</I> &gt;&gt;<P>Not much of a similarity.  Iran, Sudan, Cuba, China do the same things.<P>&lt;&lt; <I>-Taliban forces (or convinces) people to do things that they know are wrong. They justify it by what their spiritual/political leaders say is what God wants<BR>-Hitler did also but his people justified it differently. For the SS, it was "following orders."</I> &gt;&gt;<P>The Tailban preaches what they believe.  If people go along with it, they don't "know" its wrong.  They "know" its right.  Why else would they do it?  Justifying things they know are wrong doesn't make any sense. <P>&lt;&lt; <I>-The Taliban's popularity is somewhat forced. People are scared to offer any opposition, due to spiritual and physical conflicts/restraint.<BR>-The Nazi party was popular, as it offered hope, but not unanimous. When it became clear that they were very willing to ostracise anybody opposed (or different) to them, everybody waved the flag, if only to be left alone...</I> &gt;&gt;<P>so where is the similarity here?  Tailban forces popularity, Nazi's didn't do that until the late 30s.  Even then most Germans were already with them, voluntarily for the most part.<P><BR> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000023||Mach10||09-19-2001||12:54 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Umm, what countries, and what teenagers?  The Tailban showed on CNN were all old muslims with foot long beards.  How are they well organized?  If they are so well organized, how come they meet in mud huts and can't seem to make any kind of a country out of what they have.<BR>...<BR>...<BR>...<BR>The Tailban preaches what they believe.  If people go along with it, they don't "know" its wrong.  They "know" its right.  Why else would they do it?  Justifying things they know are wrong doesn't make any sense. <P>so where is the similarity here?  Tailban forces popularity, Nazi's didn't do that until the late 30s.  Even then most Germans were already with them, voluntarily for the most part.<BR>...<BR>...<BR>...</B><BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>*I shortened the quote to keep the post a little shorter   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>When the Taliban started up in 1994, it small standing ar<BR>my composed of war vets from it's previous years of infighting were swelled by an influx of Pakistani schoolchildren from the <I>madrasas</I>. They were attracted to the Taliban movement by the Taliban's commitment to bringing an end to the more-or-less constant civil war. With it's new military strength, this didn't take long.<BR>I don't recall that I said that the Taliban was extremely well-organized. I said that they were fairly well organized. I need to make that distiction. That Taliban is centralized, with none of the cells acting without the approval of the leaders. They were organized enough to pacify (sort of) a warring nation, and keep it peaceful by brutally enforcing all of their laws. <P>The old men-long beards you are seeing on TV are the spiritual leaders. They are at the top of the food chain. They don't have to get bloody, just interpret the Qua'Ran in their own unique fashion, and order the Taliban ot continue the attrocities. <P>This was bad wording on my part. The Taliban, through a combination of force and preaching would warp the perception of the people to their own version of the Qua'Ran. The Taliban aren't the majority. Most of the people, like I said earlier, are subsistance farmers/herders. Add to that a horrendously war-torn country, and you have a group of people who wnat NO more trouble in their lives. The Taliban offers relative peace, at the total expense of any form of freedom. It's better than running a plow over a minefeild. Therefore, the people will not offer much in the way of resistance. They will show up at the Mosques, bar their women indoors, and shun anything related to entertainment, all for the possibility of not being shot at daily.<BR>After speaking with an old family friend, who lived in Nazi Germany before immigrating to Canada, and showing me pictures of his young self waving a swastika at a parade, I will stand by my statement. Most of Germany was petrified of the Nazi party. In it's early stages, it was seen as a good thing, but by the time people realized differently, it had already gathered enough momentum to keep itself going. Of course there was support, almost to the end, but to say that the majority of Germans were pro-nazi is wrong.<P>In several of your other comments, you liken the Taliban's actions to several other of the extremist groups in this world's history. I will not dispute that. The Nazis weren't the first, the certainly aren't the last. That's not my argument. My argument is that the Author of the article I posted considers the Taliban to be like the Nazi party. I agree, you don't. Fair enough, I'm just telling WHY I agree. <P>Oh, and yes, I misspelt Mein Kampf   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> so did you   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>It's all good   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Keep it friendly like. We both agree. Both the Nazis and the Taliban are Evil. The Nazis killed Jews and Gay people, the Taliban kills children, women, men, actually, about anybody that doesn't do whatever they say immediately.<P>As far as "What Countries," I must admit that I overstated myself. I was fairly certain that the Taliban was active in several of the Middle-east countries. However, I can only find references to active groups in Afghanistan (where they control 90% of the country) and a considerably smaller group in Pakistan. My bad   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P><BR>Edit: Formatting, and missed point<P><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000024||EdsB52||09-19-2001||01:37 AM||||Does everyone like how Punk10 espouses this 'let's be friendly and get along' message while having recently sent me this email?<P><BR>"Who the HELL do you think you are? You think you Americans are the only people that lost friends or families? You arrogant, stupid ******* . I saw the first pictures in the morning on the news, just after<BR>getting to school. Both me, and my girlfriend curled up on the couch and cried. I STILL feel sick. I can't watch TV Canadian Apathy? **** you.  My heart wrenches EVERY time I see photos of that attack. I can't even read the news. **** you."<P><BR>I'm sure many of the words will be replaced with asterisks, but I think most will be able to discern what was sent me. I only post irrational emails that are defamatory. Many people have sent me emails that constructively disagree with me in regard to various issues that will never be posted. The purpose of me posting this is deterrence. Everyone that reads my posts knows that I don't subscribe to deterrence theory and the fact that people continue to send me the above literature is proof of my contention that deterrence doesn't work. Unless the author wants me to post his goody for some personal reason, I would think these emails would cease.<P>I've never contended that we should initiate with mass annihilation or extermination. I believe we should do whatever it takes to cease terrorism, up to and including extermination, but don't think it will be necessary. The first post of this thread posted by Punk10 was from an Internet club where someone claiming to be an Afghanistan refugee wrote some rhetoric about the radical terrorists being a tiny minority and not representative of the whole. That's good and fine, but I'd rather risk or expend millions of 'innocent' lives (Afghanistan or ?) than put America, Canada, or any other peace-loving country at risk. The tactic should be to run troops through all of those Arabic countries until we squeeze out all of the garbage. This next paragraph will be in capital letters to get the attention of Punk10.<P>PUNK10, YOU'VE ESPOUSED ALL KINDS OF CRAP ABOUT WHAT NOT TO DO; WHEN ARE YOU GOING TO EXHIBIT AN EXAMPLE OR TWO OF WHAT TACTICS WE SHOULD FOLLOW? WILL YOU QUIT CRYING BULLY AND BE A MAN THAT'S WILLING TO BE DRAFTED (IF CANADA HAS THE DRAFT) IF NECCESSARY TO FIGHT FOR FREEDOM? HERE'S A QUESTION; TO WHERE DO DRAFT-DODGERS IN CANADA RUN OFF?<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||Black88GT||09-19-2001||08:14 PM||||Umm no, its spelled M-E-I-N  K-E-M-P-F<BR> <A HREF="http://www.vintagelibrary.com/hst.cfm?y=1925" TARGET=_blank>http://www.vintagelibrary.com/hst.cfm?y=1925</A> <P><BR>Anywho.  I find it amazing how someone can comment to such extremes about a country they have neither been to or lived for any amount of time whatsoever.  I mean, I'm not going to get up here and tell you how things are in Afghanistan, because I don't know.  And if you base all your statements on what the media tells you, then you should re-evaluate your opinion forming skills.  All I can tell you about Afghanistan is the facts.  I can't tell you how the people there feel.  <P>I do know that history has shown us that an oppressed people have the ability to rise up against all odds and overcome their opressors.  If they were so disgruntled with the Taliban, they would riot/cause civil unrest, overthrow the Taliban, set up their own gov't.  Thats what the Northern Alliance is trying to do.  Why don't more Afghans join the Northern Alliance if they are against the Taliban way of life? <P>Did you say the Taliban offers peace?  Is a Civil War what you consider peace?  How about harboring terrorists, is that a peaceful way of life?  What about cutting off the legs of women if they are shown in public?  Or stoning women to death for adultery?  Is that peaceful?<P>Obviously the majority of Germans were pro-Nazi.  How did they sustain a war of that magnitude if they didn't have the majority of the german citizens on their side?  They were invading the Russians, while fighting in Africa, fighting over the skies of London, and battling in the seas of the Atlantic.  You can't do that with 49% support.<P>The Taliban is an extremist religious group, correct?  The Nazi's hated all forms of religion since they felt your first loyalty should be with Germany (Also it should be noted that the Nazi's killed more communists then Jews.  They also killed many christians.  They supported very few churches in Germany I must add to be correct, but overall they despised religion).  Thats a night and day difference. <P>This isn't an argument of opinion really.  It's an argument of the facts.  The fact is there are far more differences then similarities.<P>Also it should be noted that the Nazi's killed more communists then Jews.  They also killed many christians. <p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||09-19-2001||08:21 PM||||Hi Eds! What took you?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I'll be honest with you. I don't know what to do. But I do know what SHOULDN'T be done. Exterminating the middle east, for a start. If that's not your position, could have fooled me. From what I'm hearing, there won't be a "Gulf War" buildup in this situation, but rather, the Al-Quaida network will be targetted. That's a good step in the right direction. That email was in response to a post you had made to me. Remember? After you started making a whole bunch of false generalizations about the middle east culture. Then going on to say how the only people against wiping the arabs off the map (Yes, I am paraphrasing) were the Apathetic Canadians. I'm real apathetic. We have lost at least one good friend of the family. I'll have to check what floor she was on, but if she was OK, we would have heard from her by now. Don't you DARE presume to call me apathetic. Did YOU know anyone in those buildings? I've taken my losses, but I still see no reason in a massive retaliatory genocide campaign. You say that you weren't for mass extermination. Your posts suggested otherwise. Fine, a misunderstanding. As for my side, I feel that military action SHOULD be taken. I DO say, however, that we (yes us) should go in with scalpels, and excise the rotten bits. Leave the goat-herders and subsistance farmers alone. Don't use a chainsaw when all you need is a scalpel. I have never said "Give peace a chance" You tried that, and lost a large chunk of downtown Manhattan. My posts were against all the anti-islamic comments. No matter what has happened, it sickens me to see such an outporing of hate-literature and ignorance about a different culture. So I posted as such. I REPEAT! I am NOT against military action. I am against a non-discriminating campaign, and I am against all forms of Bigotry. The Muslim = Evil post kinda fit that bill, in my mind. The points in that post were logical, but IMHO, uncalled for, and unsupported. We disagree. Fair enough.<P>Am I willing to get conscripted? If it comes to that, yes. However, I would be useless in combat. Chronic Asthma, and horrible vision (-6.75/-7.5), but I'd be more than happy to serve any way that I was needed. Feild nurse, whatever. I wouldn't be pulling any triggers, but I wouldn't be "dodging the draft." Canada has laws against conscription, but if ****  hits fan hardcore, and Canada needs me, I'll be there, doing whatever I can. <P>I will make a public appology for that email. Eds has copied it pretty much Verbatim.<BR>Eds: <I>You caught me with your post at a rough time. All of us were feeling stressed. I had found out the previous day that an old friend of the family (she was here for dinner at Easter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) was in the trade center, at the time of the crash. Her brother called immediately after the first crash, and said that he was ok, and evacuating, but he couldn't find her. The second crash occured a few minutes later, and we haven't heard from either of them since. I was in a shitty mood, and your post got to me. We lost people too, and the post made a mockery of that. However, I appologize for the email: I'm Sorry.</I><P>I will not appologize for any of my posts. I stand behind my beliefs. Game on?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||7
Z||000027||stsmithgt||09-19-2001||08:24 PM||||Tooo much talk.  We'll probably end up trying to kill most of them anyway and let their GOD sort out good ones from evil.  I'm sure god can tell the difference.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/demon_e0.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000028||EdsB52||09-21-2001||03:38 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Hi Eds! What took you?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>[/i]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>"I'll be honest with you. I don't know what to do. But I do know what SHOULDN'T be done. Exterminating the middle east, for a start. If that's not your position, could have fooled me."<P>Show me where I wrote that the Middle East should be exterminated. The closest I said to your rendition of my assertion was that we should do whatever it takes, up to and including extermination, but the latter shouldn't be necessary. Don't cut and paste the words in my paragraphs like Tonker, your fellow Canadian did. Cut and paste entire paragraphs, or at least entire sentences, while keeping the spirit of my assertions in context, then refute away.<P>"From what I'm hearing, there won't be a "Gulf War" buildup in this situation, but rather, the Al-Quaida network will be targetted. That's a good step in the right direction."<P>Looks like you made an errant prediction, as Bush has ordered all kinds of goodies over to that region and recalled thousands of reserves.<P>"That email was in response to a post you had made to me. Remember? After you started making a whole bunch of false generalizations about the middle east culture. Then going on to say how the only people against wiping the arabs off the map (Yes, I am paraphrasing) were the Apathetic Canadians."<P>I did make generalizations about the Middle East in regard to the Muslim religion being applied in an evil fashion, and how most of the world's terrorism being exported from that region. I stand by my assertions and qualify them as opinions. Since you're too lazy to research what I wrote, but feel justified paraphrasing, as it seems close enough to the truth, I'll research. I wrote:<BR>"See, I knew all Canadians weren't about apathetic avoidance. Very good points." In thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html</A>     on page 7. Then I wrote:<BR>"Good, we're partners in commerce, now we're partners in war; welcome aboard."  To a fellow Canadian in the very next post on the same page. Then I wrote:<BR>"I understand your position; this is not your war and you don't want to get sucked into it, which seems to be a common Canadian sentiment."   a few posts later on the same page. This statement doesn't infer apathy, just a mutual desire for Canadians to keep their country out of a war, as I've previously asserted.<P>Anyway, I think you get the idea. Why don't you post written statements to reinforce your assertions instead of relying on paraphrasation and contrived interpretations?<P>"I'm real apathetic. We have lost at least one good friend of the family. I'll have to check what floor she was on, but if she was OK, we would have heard from her by now. Don't you DARE presume to call me apathetic. Did YOU know anyone in those buildings? I've taken my losses, but I still see no reason in a massive retaliatory genocide campaign."<P>So now you're trying to qualify yourself to feel as much hurt and anger as we Americans feel by disclosing a lost friend? If she indeed died in that terror, I'm truly sorry to her and to all of her loved ones. You're apathetic, now what's the ultimatum you've presented? If you indeed lost that friend, I would think you would be that much more fired up for revenge. Your statements don't compute. Are you resorting to semantics to win a point? I see no need for a massive genocide campaign either, at least to start with. This is not Viet Nam or the Gulf War where we were simply protecting a foreign interest.<P>"You say that you weren't for mass extermination. Your posts suggested otherwise. Fine, a misunderstanding."<P>It's easy to conduct a search; why not do it before making accusations?<P>"As for my side, I feel that military action SHOULD be taken. I DO say, however, that we (yes us) should go in with scalpels, and excise the rotten bits. Leave the goat-herders and subsistance farmers alone. Don't use a chainsaw when all you need is a scalpel."<P>When the terrorists and the Afghani military hide amongst the sh!t farmers, what kind of scalps do you suggest we use? I think M-60's have scalpel mounts on the end, huh?<P>"I have never said "Give peace a chance" You tried that, and lost a large chunk of downtown Manhattan."<P>Actually we (US) don't have a "Give peace a chance" policy, we have a policy where we (US) enjoy freedoms of coming and going without being cavity searched. As I just came back from Vegas, going through the airport security I was just about cavity searched. So the loving nation of Afghanistan and the loving Muslim religion has undermined the freedoms I'm used to enjoying.<P>"My posts were against all the anti-islamic comments. No matter what has happened, it sickens me to see such an outporing of hate-literature and ignorance about a different culture."<P>Ignorance? What do you know about the philosophies of the Muslim people? More importantly, what do you know about the application of their religion? The only way to really know would be to be a citizen in countries where Muslims practice. So your contention of ignorance must be mutual. Don't go looking up the Muslim religion in your encyclopedia or on the Internet and act like it came off the top of your head.<P>"So I posted as such. I REPEAT! I am NOT against military action. I am against a non-discriminating campaign, and I am against all forms of Bigotry."<P>Me too. Anything wearing a rag should be a target, without prejudice! Kidding. What we need to do is to rake the region for militant resistance and eliminate it.<P>"The Muslim = Evil post kinda fit that bill, in my mind. The points in that post were logical, but IMHO, uncalled for, and unsupported. We disagree. Fair enough."<P>Unsupported? That's because they were opinions. Opinions can be supported if you want to upgrade them to argument status. <P>"I will make a public appology for that email. Eds has copied it pretty much Verbatim."<P>See, you guys can't even tell the truth when you're caught. I cut and pasted that post absolutely verbatim. The only difference between what I read and what is posted are the asterisks, which I can't do anything about. If you or anyone wants me to forward the email sent from you to me, I would be glad to do it.<P>"Eds: [i]You caught me with your post at a rough time. All of us were feeling stressed. I had found out the previous day that an old friend of the family (she was here for dinner at Easter   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) was in the trade center, at the time of the crash. Her brother called immediately after the first crash, and said that he was ok, and evacuating, but he couldn't find her. The second crash occured a few minutes later, and we haven't heard from either of them since. I was in a shitty mood, and your post got to me. We lost people too, and the post made a mockery of that. However, I appologize for the email: I'm Sorry."<P>This doesn't make sense. My post was in support of, A) payback, and B) prevention at any and all costs, how is that making a mockery of your potentially fallen friend?||||reg||1
Z||000029||Mach10||09-21-2001||04:52 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by EdsB52:<P>"Show me where I wrote that the Middle East should be exterminated. The closest I said to your rendition of my assertion was that we should do whatever it takes, up to and including extermination, but the latter shouldn't be necessary. Don't cut and paste the words in my paragraphs like Tonker, your fellow Canadian did. Cut and paste entire paragraphs, or at least entire sentences, while keeping the spirit of my assertions in context, then refute away.<P>[quote]<BR><B>I like how their favorite expression is "The white man is the devil."</B> I'm into freedom of expression, but <B>I think we, in America, need a little caveat that states towel wearing Muslims don't get to exercise that freedom.</B> Christianity can be oppressive, restrictive, and intrusive, especially when combined with legislation, but <B>Muslims are all about believe what we believe or die.</B> Freedom of expression ends way before extermination for non-compliance. Some might say that my beliefs are no better than those of the terrorists. My answer parallels GW's sentiments; the war has changed and the rules have changed, so we must change with them. <B>If we need to exterminate an entire race of people that want to exterminate us, then we should, regardless of humanity restraints.</B> America has always borne the burden of acting more "human" that other nations and I'm sick of that requirement. <I>This is not Viet Nam, a war driven by politics; this is a contemporary version of a quasi-WWII, which is quite different in that we carry little to no blame for its construction. We are allies with Israel, but that's quite different from policing and warring with a foreign nation.</I><P>We'll see if my bold-facing highlights the right phrases:<BR>The bold-face statements are what I take exception to. I'll readily admit that I saw red when I read the first half. I didn't read the second half to well, which does spin the argument into more neutral territory(in italics). Very well. I will retract my "Bigot" comment.<P>"Looks like you made an errant prediction, as Bush has ordered all kinds of goodies over to that region and recalled thousands of reserves."<BR>-Well, I'm repeating what I'm seeing and hearing. Official statements, news from different parts of the world etc. Your administration has released statements about how they are specifically targetting the Al-Qaieda (yet another, probably WRONG spelling :rolleyes) Network. The US HAS to establish a sound military footing, a good base, and easily deployed backup. That doesn't necessarily mean that they are going to be using everything that's moving out there.  On the other hand, if the Taliban movement (who by the way are mostly responsible for the bad press surrounding muslims for the past 7 or so years) makes good on it's promise to start ****  with it's neighbors, it would be a good idea to have all those toys in the area.<P>"I did make generalizations about the Middle East in regard to the Muslim religion being applied in an evil fashion, and how most of the world's terrorism being exported from that region. I stand by my assertions and qualify them as opinions. Since you're too lazy to research what I wrote, but feel justified paraphrasing, as it seems close enough to the truth, I'll research. I wrote:<BR>"See, I knew all Canadians weren't about apathetic avoidance. Very good points." In thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html</A>     on page 7. Then I wrote:<BR>"Good, we're partners in commerce, now we're partners in war; welcome aboard."  To a fellow Canadian in the very next post on the same page. Then I wrote:<BR>"I understand your position; this is not your war and you don't want to get sucked into it, which seems to be a common Canadian sentiment."   a few posts later on the same page. This statement doesn't infer apathy, just a mutual desire for Canadians to keep their country out of a war, as I've previously asserted.<P>Anyway, I think you get the idea. Why don't you post written statements to reinforce your assertions instead of relying on paraphrasation and contrived interpretations?"<BR>-The post I was refering to was <I>"Another Canuck in dissent, what a surprise. YOU ARE NOT QUALIFIED TO BE PISSED. I hope I don't need to tell that to another Canadian! Why is it all the Canadians want to be touchy-feely about this? Could it be they didn't loose so much today? Could it be they want the whole matter to blow over? Could it be that they are never the targets of terrorism? Or could it be all of these?"</I><BR>That is the post which got to me, Eds. Now, I made the error of stating that this particular post was made at me. It wasn't, it was directed at Brandon. The rest of the posts on Page 6 were spouting the same sentiment. It *is* true that you took some of these back on page 7, but only after Voytek wrote this: <I>While only SOME of the people there may be friendly, the majority of them (and that includes the 'opressed' women) are hateful towards anyone non-muslim.<P>While only SOME of the people there may be friendly, the majority of them (and that includes the 'opressed' women) are hateful towards anyone non-muslim.</I><BR>I didn't comment to Voytek, as I had missed that particular post. But in essence, you were saying that he was not apathetic by stating that most muslims hated the west. I didn't hold much for this turnaround simply because Voytek was making the same generalizations about a culture that you had, initially. My take on this was that Canadians were only "ok" if they were spouting the same bigotry. <P>"So now you're trying to qualify yourself to feel as much hurt and anger as we Americans feel by disclosing a lost friend? If she indeed died in that terror, I'm truly sorry to her and to all of her loved ones. You're apathetic, now what's the ultimatum you've presented? If you indeed lost that friend, I would think you would be that much more fired up for revenge. Your statements don't compute. Are you resorting to semantics to win a point? I see no need for a massive genocide campaign either, at least to start with. This is not Viet Nam or the Gulf War where we were simply protecting a foreign interest."<BR>-Well, yes. I am saying that I am entitled to the same outrage. You lost countrymen, so did I. This friend was visiting her brother, like I said. She was one of the 3 (known) Canadians lost. Her brother is a US citizen. I feel 2 losses. Loss of 2 countrypeople, and the loss of one friend (to be honest I'd never met her brother  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ). So you ask why am I not more vengeful, or hateful to islam? Bear in mind, I am studying Anthropology. Physical Anthro is my Major, Cultural is my minor. I'm a few credits short of my BA. What does this tell you about me? I STUDY PEOPLE. Cultural Anthropology is about holism, and about cross-cultural comparison. I have to look for similarities and differences in people. I try my best not to be ethnocentric in my views. This in effect means that I try not to subscribe to stereotypes, and that I remain impartial to other societies. Middle east included. So I see myself as an enlightened individual, free of any racial prejudices. So when something like this happens, I don't blame muslims. I blame the crazy assholes on the planes, and the people who put them there. I find the growing hate of muslims sickening. So I don't subscribe to it. <BR>I do agree, this conflict is like nothing the US has been involved with since Japan.<P>"It's easy to conduct a search; why not do it before making accusations?"<BR>No, I read the posts, that was my overall take. I just didn't feel like posting them again. I re-read the offending articles, and I feel the same still. However, having read more carefully, they AREN'T as bad. You have to stop that, Eds. You make EXCELLENT points, but bury them deep. Not everyone has enough time between calls to slog through your essays...<P><BR>"When the terrorists and the Afghani military hide amongst the sh!t farmers, what kind of scalps do you suggest we use? I think M-60's have scalpel mounts on the end, huh?"<BR>-Er, Bayonettes?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Actually, if you're after the extremist military, go after the Taliban. They'll be more than happy to run shrieking at the US soldiers, holding guns backwards. It's the slimy terrorists who will hide behind the sh!t farmers. But without a fanatical drooling military power behind them, they'll just be a bunch of clueless assholes, no different from any other frothing schizophrenic.<P>"Actually we (US) don't have a "Give peace a chance" policy, we have a policy where we (US) enjoy freedoms of coming and going without being cavity searched. As I just came back from Vegas, going through the airport security I was just about cavity searched. So the loving nation of Afghanistan and the loving Muslim religion has undermined the freedoms I'm used to enjoying."<BR>-That was my point. Before this, airport security was relaxed, and people were going about their business. That all changed with this attack. Wording was wrong, but my point rests...<P>"Ignorance? What do you know about the philosophies of the Muslim people? More importantly, what do you know about the application of their religion? The only way to really know would be to be a citizen in countries where Muslims practice. So your contention of ignorance must be mutual. Don't go looking up the Muslim religion in your encyclopedia or on the Internet and act like it came off the top of your head."<BR>-It DIDN'T come off the top of my head. I've read a sizeable chunk of the Qua'Ran, I have SEVERAL islamic friends who are sick and TIRED of me talking to them about this. I don't speak out of ignorance. Islam is NOT about waging war on white people or non-believers. The Taliban would love for it to say that. It would save them the trouble of having to re-write it everytime someone comes across an inconsistency in their own personalized versions. The literacy rate in Afghanistan is around 10% The people there are being told what to believe by people who say that it is the word of God. Who else will they believe?<P>"Me too. Anything wearing a rag should be a target, without prejudice! Kidding. What we need to do is to rake the region for militant resistance and eliminate it."<BR>-Touche.<P>"Unsupported? That's because they were opinions. Opinions can be supported if you want to upgrade them to argument status."<BR>-Fair enough, opinions. But it still looks like hate-literature to me. <P>"See, you guys can't even tell the truth when you're caught. I cut and pasted that post absolutely verbatim. The only difference between what I read and what is posted are the asterisks, which I can't do anything about. If you or anyone wants me to forward the email sent from you to me, I would be glad to do it."<BR>-Caught? I'm not ashamed of that email. Anyone wanting a CC can PM me for it. You didn't catch me. You posted a private email. Oh, well. I didn't have to explain myself, but I did. The email was what it was: Possibly innapropriate, yes. Defamatory? Not really, but it did use a lot of 4-letter words. You weren't clear wether YOU had removed the choice language, or had let UBB do it. More or less verbatim, less the actual spellin of the word ****.<P>"This doesn't make sense. My post was in support of, A) payback, and B) prevention at any and all costs, how is that making a mockery of your potentially fallen friend?"<BR>-Eds: There were 2 issues that led to that email. First, was your Muslim = evil post. I am fanatically against racism in all forms, so you had me going. I was pissed off there. They you posted how Canadians were apathetic because they hadn't lost anything. I flipped, and wrote that little masterpiece. You posted, and I appologized. If you had emailed me personally with your previous post, I like to think I would have appologized in email form as well. This isn't the first time something like this happened. I appologized via email then, and we were cool until now. I can guarantee you, us being the volatile characters that we are, that this won't be the last time that we will be shouting at each other. I don't hold grudges (unless you got me for lunch money in gr. 5). I was pissed, now I'm not. I said I was sorry for the email, and I am. You think that possibly the only solution to the middle east problems might be military action, up to and including the pacifying(to put it lightly) the entire region. I don't think so. I think that accurate strikes to the right people will do it. We can agree to disagree, and argue back and forth as usual, with no inherent malice, or we can drop it. You are a well-read individual, and it IS a challenge to research my replies. If nothing else, it gives me something to do while I'm at work waiting for the next retard to call me telling me that his cuphold on his PC is broken again.<P>So what do you say? Do we call a truce, and stop going at each other personally, but continue more civilized discussion?<P><BR>Black88GT: I have things I want to say, but I am FAR too tired right now. Look here tommorrow evening....<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000030||EdsB52||09-21-2001||02:33 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>[QUOTE]Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>OBVIOUS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #1:<BR>“"Show me where I wrote that the Middle East should be exterminated. The closest I said to your rendition of my assertion was that we should do whatever it takes, up to and including extermination, but the latter shouldn't be necessary. Don't cut and paste the words in my paragraphs like Tonker, your fellow Canadian did. Cut and paste entire paragraphs, or at least entire sentences, while keeping the spirit of my assertions in context, then refute away.”<P>[quote]<BR><B>I like how their favorite expression is "The white man is the devil."</B> I'm into freedom of expression, but <B>I think we, in America, need a little caveat that states towel wearing Muslims don't get to exercise that freedom.</B> Christianity can be oppressive, restrictive, and intrusive, especially when combined with legislation, but <B>Muslims are all about believe what we believe or die.</B> Freedom of expression ends way before extermination for non-compliance. Some might say that my beliefs are no better than those of the terrorists. My answer parallels GW's sentiments; the war has changed and the rules have changed, so we must change with them. <B>If we need to exterminate an entire race of people that want to exterminate us, then we should, regardless of humanity restraints.</B> America has always borne the burden of acting more "human" that other nations and I'm sick of that requirement. <I>This is not Viet Nam, a war driven by politics; this is a contemporary version of a quasi-WWII, which is quite different in that we carry little to no blame for its construction. We are allies with Israel, but that's quite different from policing and warring with a foreign nation.</I><P>We'll see if my bold-facing highlights the right phrases:<BR>The bold-face statements are what I take exception to. I'll readily admit that I saw red when I read the first half. I didn't read the second half to well, which does spin the argument into more neutral territory(in italics). Very well. I will retract my "Bigot" comment."<P>Oh, so you systematically ignore part of the intent of my statement. Are you and Tonker swapping spit? The second half is where I establish the all-important operative word that denotes the “need” to perform such acts. “Need” changes the entire intent from recreational, hate, or bigotry, as you’ve asserted. “Need’ establishes a requirement, not justification for dislike. Besides, you’re so vague. In the first half I denounced Muslims and Christians for different reasons, quoted some of my least favorite people that utter “The white man is the devil,” and wrote, “Freedom of expression ends way before extermination for non-compliance.” The extermination for which I was referring was the apparent extermination of Americans by terrorist groups from that region. The preceding statement establishes that here, “Christianity can be oppressive, restrictive, and intrusive, especially when combined with legislation, but [b]Muslims are all about believe what we believe or die.” The freedom of expression for which I was obviously referring was the right to express what you want as long as you keep it within your own parameters and not subjugate others into assuming your beliefs. You can’t understand. Correct me if I’m wrong, but Canada is mono-racial for the most part, right? If so, do you have any form of affirmative action? If so, is it intrusive? It’s much easier for a mono-race society to get along with each other. Like I said, you can’t understand. I don’t have a problem with a diverse society, but it does lend to problems for which you don’t/can’t understand. It’s amazing at how you highlight various words instead of leaving them in plain textual form, and then openly admit you focused on one part of the paragraph instead of taking the entire meaning. So what you said is that you agree that the message was as I delivered.<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #2:<P>"Looks like you made an errant prediction, as Bush has ordered all kinds of goodies over to that region and recalled thousands of reserves."<BR>-Well, I'm repeating what I'm seeing and hearing. Official statements, news from different parts of the world etc. Your administration has released statements about how they are specifically targetting the Al-Qaieda (yet another, probably WRONG spelling :rolleyes) Network. The US HAS to establish a sound military footing, a good base, and easily deployed backup. That doesn't necessarily mean that they are going to be using everything that's moving out there.  On the other hand, if the Taliban movement (who by the way are mostly responsible for the bad press surrounding muslims for the past 7 or so years) makes good on it's promise to start ****  with it's neighbors, it would be a good idea to have all those toys in the area.<P>That was in response to you writing this:<BR>"From what I'm hearing, there won't be a "Gulf War" buildup in this situation, but rather, the Al-Quaida network will be targetted. That's a good step in the right direction."<BR>Are you saying we aren’t building up our arms in that region? A military buildup is where we stack an area with war toys, what part of that isn’t happening? You’re confusing (purposely to avoid being wrong again) military buildup with all out slaughter. You can buildup the military in an area without slaughtering, but it just isn’t as much fun! Who said anything about using everything that’s moving out there? See, you take things out of context and fabricate what you need to satisfy your fallacious arguments. It’s simple; a military buildup, as I’ve stated, is nothing more than a collection of military weapons. Don’t read into it that a military buildup is an exercise where we attack people; that’s an offensive. The military buildup is the element of the entire operation where we locate suitable weaponry to a given area. We can target anything, everything, or nothing that moves, and not have that element of the operation be considered part of the buildup. You use the word “footing” as a metaphor for buildup. Quit dancing around the truth; you have no clue what you’re talking about.<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #3:<P>"I did make generalizations about the Middle East in regard to the Muslim religion being applied in an evil fashion, and how most of the world's terrorism being exported from that region. I stand by my assertions and qualify them as opinions. Since you're too lazy to research what I wrote, but feel justified paraphrasing, as it seems close enough to the truth, I'll research. I wrote:<BR>"See, I knew all Canadians weren't about apathetic avoidance. Very good points." In thread <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum6/HTML/003326-7.html</A>     on page 7. Then I wrote:<BR>"Good, we're partners in commerce, now we're partners in war; welcome aboard."  To a fellow Canadian in the very next post on the same page. Then I wrote:<BR>"I understand your position; this is not your war and you don't want to get sucked into it, which seems to be a common Canadian sentiment."   a few posts later on the same page. This statement doesn't infer apathy, just a mutual desire for Canadians to keep their country out of a war, as I've previously asserted.<P>Anyway, I think you get the idea. Why don't you post written statements to reinforce your assertions instead of relying on paraphrasation and contrived interpretations?"<BR>-The post I was refering to was <I>"Another Canuck in dissent, what a surprise. YOU ARE NOT QUALIFIED TO BE PISSED. I hope I don't need to tell that to another Canadian! Why is it all the Canadians want to be touchy-feely about this? Could it be they didn't loose so much today? Could it be they want the whole matter to blow over? Could it be that they are never the targets of terrorism? Or could it be all of these?"</I><BR>That is the post which got to me, Eds. Now, I made the error of stating that this particular post was made at me. It wasn't, it was directed at Brandon. The rest of the posts on Page 6 were spouting the same sentiment. It *is* true that you took some of these back on page 7, but only after Voytek wrote this: <I>While only SOME of the people there may be friendly, the majority of them (and that includes the 'opressed' women) are hateful towards anyone non-muslim.<P>While only SOME of the people there may be friendly, the majority of them (and that includes the 'opressed' women) are hateful towards anyone non-muslim.</I><BR>I didn't comment to Voytek, as I had missed that particular post. But in essence, you were saying that he was not apathetic by stating that most muslims hated the west. I didn't hold much for this turnaround simply because Voytek was making the same generalizations about a culture that you had, initially. My take on this was that Canadians were only "ok" if they were spouting the same bigotry.<BR>You’ve misconstruen yet another of my sentiments. This post started out by you accusing me of calling all Canadians apathetic, and ended with you inserting “ok” as my feelings toward Canadians if they are “spouting the same bigotry.” What you’re trying to say is that I endorse all Canadians that share my feelings on the action that should be taken. I would like to expand that to the fact that I endorse all people all over the world that share my feelings. I denounce people with your feelings. What I’ve found is that some people that aren’t Americans don’t feel the sense of urgency that Americans do to settle this matter once and for all. If this event occurred in Canada, France, or England, I would be just as fired up about doing what it takes to remedy this matter, up to and including total annihilation if necessary. What I’ve found is an apathetic attitude from non-Americans in that they don’t want to involve their country and sacrifice their citizens in a war they don’t want. I call that apathy. As for Voytek, he’s the “man” element for which I was referring in one of my posts when I wrote something like there are men in Canada, so I don’t want to generalize all Canadians as apathetic. I stand by that assertion. And you didn’t comment to Voytek because you were afraid he would come over and put the smack down on your ass. Or is it country discrimination? You won’t slam a fellow countryman for espousing the same beliefs that I do; sounds like a favoritism or fear.<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #4:<P>"So now you're trying to qualify yourself to feel as much hurt and anger as we Americans feel by disclosing a lost friend? If she indeed died in that terror, I'm truly sorry to her and to all of her loved ones. You're apathetic, now what's the ultimatum you've presented? If you indeed lost that friend, I would think you would be that much more fired up for revenge. Your statements don't compute. Are you resorting to semantics to win a point? I see no need for a massive genocide campaign either, at least to start with. This is not Viet Nam or the Gulf War where we were simply protecting a foreign interest."<BR>-Well, yes. I am saying that I am entitled to the same outrage. You lost countrymen, so did I. This friend was visiting her brother, like I said. She was one of the 3 (known) Canadians lost. Her brother is a US citizen. I feel 2 losses. Loss of 2 countrypeople, and the loss of one friend (to be honest I'd never met her brother    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ). So you ask why am I not more vengeful, or hateful to islam? Bear in mind, I am studying Anthropology. Physical Anthro is my Major, Cultural is my minor. I'm a few credits short of my BA. What does this tell you about me? I STUDY PEOPLE. Cultural Anthropology is about holism, and about cross-cultural comparison. I have to look for similarities and differences in people. I try my best not to be ethnocentric in my views. This in effect means that I try not to subscribe to stereotypes, and that I remain impartial to other societies. Middle east included. So I see myself as an enlightened individual, free of any racial prejudices. So when something like this happens, I don't blame muslims. I blame the crazy assholes on the planes, and the people who put them there. I find the growing hate of muslims sickening. So I don't subscribe to it. <BR>I do agree, this conflict is like nothing the US has been involved with since Japan.”<BR>Even though this event did include people from all over the world, it occurred on American soil. Though I’m not trying to mitigate Canada’s loss, 3 doesn’t equal 6,000. If a salesman were murdered on your doorstep in a drive-by shooting, you would be much more angry than if he were killed on the street corner at the end of your block. You would feel less anger if it happened across town. Do you understand the personal nature of this?<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #5:<P>“"It's easy to conduct a search; why not do it before making accusations?"<BR>No, I read the posts, that was my overall take. I just didn't feel like posting them again. I re-read the offending articles, and I feel the same still. However, having read more carefully, they AREN'T as bad. You have to stop that, Eds. You make EXCELLENT points, but bury them deep. Not everyone has enough time between calls to slog through your essays...”<BR>Overall take? Then why did you dichotomize the #1 misinterpretation’s paragraph? Overall means the entirety, but you segregated the meaning in the first post and now claim you read and weigh the entire paragraph. Pick a side of the fence. Didn’t feel like posting again? That’s why I call you apathetic. Slog through? Your post isn’t long?<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #6:<P>“"Actually we (US) don't have a "Give peace a chance" policy, we have a policy where we (US) enjoy freedoms of coming and going without being cavity searched. As I just came back from Vegas, going through the airport security I was just about cavity searched. So the loving nation of Afghanistan and the loving Muslim religion has undermined the freedoms I'm used to enjoying."<BR>-That was my point. Before this, airport security was relaxed, and people were going about their business. That all changed with this attack. Wording was wrong, but my point rests...”<BR>You accused America of having a "Give peace a chance" policy. Peace and freedom are different things.<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #7:<P>“"Ignorance? What do you know about the philosophies of the Muslim people? More importantly, what do you know about the application of their religion? The only way to really know would be to be a citizen in countries where Muslims practice. So your contention of ignorance must be mutual. Don't go looking up the Muslim religion in your encyclopedia or on the Internet and act like it came off the top of your head."<BR>-It DIDN'T come off the top of my head. I've read a sizeable chunk of the Qua'Ran, I have SEVERAL islamic friends who are sick and TIRED of me talking to them about this. I don't speak out of ignorance. Islam is NOT about waging war on white people or non-believers. The Taliban would love for it to say that. It would save them the trouble of having to re-write it everytime someone comes across an inconsistency in their own personalized versions. The literacy rate in Afghanistan is around 10% The people there are being told what to believe by people who say that it is the word of God. Who else will they believe?”<P>If you’ve done such extensive reading then you must have avoided the cover of those books where it’s spelled Qur’an, not Qua’ran. Don’t give me this sh!t of a one-time misspelling because I’ve noticed you always spell it that way. That’s equal to writing Jesis Krist. If you’re such a frickin authority, then why can’t you spell it correctly? Just use the easier version; Koran. What I’m saying is the application of the Qur’an is for evil to white people, not the intended preachings.<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #8:<P>“"Me too. Anything wearing a rag should be a target, without prejudice! Kidding. What we need to do is to rake the region for militant resistance and eliminate it."<BR>-Touche.”<BR>How is that a touché?<P>OBVIUOS MISINTERPRETATION BY MACH 10 #9:<P>“"See, you guys can't even tell the truth when you're caught. I cut and pasted that post absolutely verbatim. The only difference between what I read and what is posted are the asterisks, which I can't do anything about. If you or anyone wants me to forward the email sent from you to me, I would be glad to do it."<BR>-Caught? I'm not ashamed of that email. Anyone wanting a CC can PM me for it. You didn't catch me. You posted a private email. Oh, well. I didn't have to explain myself, but I did. The email was what it was: Possibly innapropriate, yes. Defamatory? Not really, but it did use a lot of 4-letter words. You weren't clear wether YOU had removed the choice language, or had let UBB do it. More or less verbatim, less the actual spellin of the word ****.”<BR>I don’t care about the message content of the email, but what I’m referring to is the notion that you stated it was pretty much verbatim. It was absolutely verbatim, but the UBB changed certain letters into asterisks, which I asserted would happen as I cut and pasted the email. The desired effect you were hoping for was the ‘pretty much verbatim’ aspect. You were leaving the door open to the possibility that I modified the post instead of accepting the post as it was, which was in its original form.<BR>“So what do you say? Do we call a truce, and stop going at each other personally, but continue more civilized discussion?”<BR>Ya. I wasn’t offended by any of your writings and don’t need an apology. You can say (write) what you want to me. Honestly, try keeping people’s statements in context. One funny irony I’ve noticed here is that Archie and I are on the same page, but he won’t dare make comments in support of anyone’s writings that mirror mine.<p>[This message has been edited by EdsB52 (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Voytek||09-21-2001||04:25 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> This has turned into an EdsB52 and Mach10 thread.<P>Let's settle this.  You guys argue over specific meanings of words and contexts and you're really splitting hairs.<P>I think both of you are somewhat right.  Eds is more of a nationalist American and Mach is more of a multiculturalism-brain-washed Canadian.  Yes, there IS a middle here somewhere.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Also it should be noted that the Nazi's killed more communists then Jews. They also killed many christians.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Amen to that Black88GT.  MILLIONS of people from other nations were exterminated by the Nazis (at least 3 million Poles and many, many others) besides the Jews.  And it took many German deaths (civilian and military) to end the atrocities.  Is this what it'll take to end terrorism?  Since it is a little hard to tell who is a terrorist and who is not down there, I think we should get ready for some casualties and stop whining about the poor civilians (most of them despise us anyway).<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000032||Mach10||09-22-2001||04:30 AM||||Voytek: Point taken. I'll try and wrap up here  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> I take offence to "multiculturalism brain-washed canadian" For one thing, it doesn't make a good acronym  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"><P>I prefer "Culturally relativistic Anthropological-Zombie" Call me "CRA-Z"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I would agree with most of them not liking them. But remember that illiteracy is at around 90%. The only things they know about Western culture have been vomitted up by the Taliban which is NOT a fan of us. <P>Eds: Misinterpretations? Obviously yes. You explained what you meant, I can accept that. In my defence, you weren't trying too hard to be understood any other way. Judging by the posts in <I>that</I> thread, a lot of other people misinterpreted them too. But whatever <I>L'agua passada no tourne la molina</I><P>"Piss under the bridge won't turn the millstone"<P>The only thing I feel the need to explain myself is that I had seen the "Qur'an" spelling a couple of times, but I don't really read words. It's hard to explain. I'll start reading a book, "Witches Abroad" for example, and be totally convinced that the lead character's name was "Margrat." In fact, it was "Magrat" It wasn't until the <B>4th</B> book, that I noticed that I had gotten it wrong... I read very fast, compared to my peers. I don't read words, I seem to read sentences... I don't really read each word, I kind of look at the line, and the meaning blinks up...  Does that make sense? Means I can finish a 400 page novel in a couple of hours. Problem is, I tend to miss details, or else mis-interpret them. So far, though, my accuracy is PRETTY good. I haven't seen a need to change it just yet. Reading the thing twice usually clears up the inconsistencies. I've read countless articles about Islam in the last week and a bit (nothing else to do at work), and I didn't notice the spelling once... So yes, it's "Qur'an.<P>On the subject of Voytek, I hadn't actually SEEN his post until after you quoted it, and I hadn't read that until a few days after. I had just stopped reading the thread, and by the time I saw it, Cliff had closed the thread. I wasn't going to start a new topic...<P>As for the countrymen thing, I still feel justified. I feel for the americans that were lost just as strongly as the Canadians that were lost. Hell, technically I knew one of each that died, though I don't remember the American, my parents assure me that he was at one of their functions in the past year or so... great, my story changes EVERY time I tell it. That's GREAT for credibility  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> . I tell you in all honesty that if I *was* a citizen, I'd feel the same way. That's just me. I can't speak for other Canadians, but I feel the loss just as harshly. I do feel the want for quick justice, but I also feel that whatever action is taken should be properly carried out, and above all else, <I>targetted at the right people</I>. I am seeing some of the same apathy thing all across the board, though, in Canada, AND the US. Admittedly, more in Canada... There is a large sentiment to the effect of "Let's just all be friends, and make peace!" To that, I say BOLLOCKS. The crime was committed, justice must prevail. Afer that, we can talk about peace. I only ask that justice may be exacted on those that deserve it. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>What I’m saying is the application of the Qur’an is for evil to white people, not the intended preachings.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I agree 100%. The teachings of the extremist groups are not good. If that was the intent of the muslim=evil post, then I can accept that with open arms. I read it again, and still don't really see it. Thank you for the clarification, it kinda changes everything...<P>The Touche comment (can't be bothered with accents  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> ) was to THIS: What we need to do is to rake the region for militant resistance and eliminate it. That is what I am pretty much saying. beat the bush, and hack up the snakes that come slithering out. Don't just set fire to the feilds...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Finally, the "pretty much Verbatim" comment wasn't intended to say that you may have changed it. What I was saying was that you had posted almost exactly what I sent you, minus the fun words. I wasn't calling you a liar  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>It's 3 am, if there's more to say, I'm too tired to do it coherently... Maybe later...||||reg||1
